    <h1>UNIT 8: LET'S CELEBRATE!<br>Lesson 9: Grammar SB - page 88</h1>

    <h2>I. OBJECTIVES</h2>
    <p>By the end of this lesson, students will be able to:</p>
    
    <p class="bold">1.1. Language knowledge and skills</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Use <b>say</b> and <b>tell</b> correctly in reported speech.</li>
        <li>Understand and apply the structure for <b>Reported yes / no questions</b> (using <i>if</i>).</li>
        <li>Report stories and dialogues dynamically.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="bold">1.2. Competences</p>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Communication and Collaboration:</b> Work in pairs/groups to report dialogues and engage in roleplay (listening to peer questions).</li>
        <li><b>Critical Thinking:</b> Effectively convert sentences back and forth between direct speech and reported speech.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="bold">1.3. Attributes</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Diligence and attention to grammatical details (especially pronouns and tense backshifting).</li>
        <li>Active listening when interacting with classmates' questions.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>II. TEACHING AIDS AND LEARNING MATERIALS</h2>
    <p><b>1. Teacher’s aids:</b> Projector / Interactive Whiteboard / TV, PowerPoint slides, Student Book, Teacher's Book.</p>
    <p><b>2. Students’ aids:</b> Student’s book (page 88), notebook, pens.</p>

    <h2>III. ASSESSMENT EVIDENCE</h2>
    <table>
        <tr>
            <th>Performance Tasks</th>
            <th>Performance Products</th>
            <th>Assessment Tools</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Warm up: Memory chain - reporting what the previous student said.</td>
            <td>Verbal responses and sentence structures.</td>
            <td>T’s observation and on-the-spot correction.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Task 1, 2, 4: Complete blanks and transform sentences using <i>say/tell</i> and reported questions.</td>
            <td>Ss’ answers in notebooks / spoken answers.</td>
            <td>T’s feedback / Peers’ feedback.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Task 3: Reverse engineering - Write direct questions from reported speech.</td>
            <td>Ss’ written questions on the board/notebooks.</td>
            <td>T’s feedback.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Task 5 & 6: Production Roleplay (British school scenario).</td>
            <td>Ss’ answers to interview questions & reported sentences.</td>
            <td>T’s observation / Peers’ feedback.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h2>IV. PROCEDURES</h2>
    
    <p class="bold">A. Warm-up (5’)</p>
    <p>a) <b>Objective:</b> Introduce the lesson topic and activate prior knowledge of Reported Speech.</p>
    <p>b) <b>Content:</b> A chain game practicing reported speech.</p>
    <p>c) <b>Expected outcomes:</b> Ss freshen up their memory on how to shift tenses and pronouns.</p>
    <p>d) <b>Organization of the activity:</b></p>
    <table>
        <tr>
            <th style="width: 50%;">TEACHER’S ACTIVITIES</th>
            <th style="width: 50%;">STUDENTS’ ACTIVITIES</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>
                - Explain instructions: <i>"Each student prepares a statement. Stand up. Student A says a sentence, Student B reports it, says a new sentence, and points to Student C..."</i><br>
                - Example: A says <i>"I like football."</i> B says <i>"He said he liked football."</i><br>
                - Monitor and assist with tense shifts.
            </td>
            <td>
                - Prepare a short sentence about themselves.<br>
                - Listen carefully to the previous student.<br>
                - Report their statement accurately and sit down.
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <p class="bold">B. New Lesson (35’)</p>
    
    <p class="indent"><b>1. Presentation: rules of Say/Tell & Yes/No Questions</b></p>
    <p class="indent">a) <b>Objective:</b> Understand the structural difference between say/tell and how to report Yes/No questions.</p>
    <p class="indent">b) <b>Content:</b> Grammar rules presentation.</p>
    <p class="indent">c) <b>Organization of the activity:</b></p>
    <table>
        <tr>
            <th style="width: 50%;">TEACHER’S ACTIVITIES</th>
            <th style="width: 50%;">STUDENTS’ ACTIVITIES</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>
                - Elicit the differences: <b>tell</b> + person/object + (that), <b>say</b> + (that).<br>
                - Explain Reported Yes/No questions: <b>ask + if + S + V(backshifted)</b>.<br>
                - Emphasize the REMEMBER box: We can leave out the object pronoun after <i>ask</i>, but NEVER after <i>tell</i>.
            </td>
            <td>
                - Listen and note down the structures.<br>
                - Give quick examples to demonstrate understanding.
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <p class="indent"><b>2. Practice: Tasks 1 to 4</b></p>
    <p class="indent">a) <b>Objective:</b> Apply rules through guided exercises.</p>
    <p class="indent">b) <b>Content:</b> Textbook tasks on page 88.</p>
    <p class="indent">c) <b>Organization of the activity:</b></p>
    <table>
        <tr>
            <th style="width: 50%;">TEACHER’S ACTIVITIES</th>
            <th style="width: 50%;">STUDENTS’ ACTIVITIES</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>
                - <b>Task 1:</b> Ask Ss to fill in the "Mud Madness" blog with <i>say/said</i> or <i>tell/told</i>.<br>
                - <b>Task 2:</b> Have Ss report dialogues (using <i>say</i> in part 1, <i>tell</i> in part 2).<br>
                - <b>Task 3:</b> Challenge Ss to write DIRECT questions based on reported sentences (e.g. <i>'Do you like horror films?' asked George.</i>).<br>
                - <b>Task 4:</b> Instruct Ss to report the provided Yes/No questions.
            </td>
            <td>
                - Work individually or in pairs to complete the tasks.<br>
                - Cross-check answers with their partners.<br>
                - Provide answers to the class and correct any mistakes regarding pronouns or tenses.
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <p class="indent"><b>3. Production: Tasks 5 & 6</b></p>
    <p class="indent">a) <b>Objective:</b> Use reported speech in a realistic communicative context.</p>
    <p class="indent">b) <b>Content:</b> Listening and reporting (British school scenario).</p>
    <p class="indent">c) <b>Organization of the activity:</b></p>
    <table>
        <tr>
            <th style="width: 50%;">TEACHER’S ACTIVITIES</th>
            <th style="width: 50%;">STUDENTS’ ACTIVITIES</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>
                - <b>Task 5:</b> Set the scene: <i>"Imagine you're at a British school..."</i> Play the conversation script and have Ss write their answers.<br>
                - <b>Task 6:</b> Ask Ss to combine the student names and questions, then write the reported questions (e.g. <i>Jonathan asked me if I liked English food.</i>)
            </td>
            <td>
                - Listen to the audio / reading of the script.<br>
                - Write down their personal responses to the questions.<br>
                - Formulate reported questions in their notebooks.
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <p class="bold">C. Consolidation & Wrap-up (5’)</p>
    <p>a) <b>Objective:</b> Consolidate learning and assign homework.</p>
    <p>b) <b>Content:</b> True/False check and assignment.</p>
    <p>c) <b>Expected outcomes:</b> Ss identify common errors correctly.</p>
    <p>d) <b>Organization of the activity:</b></p>
    <table>
        <tr>
            <th style="width: 50%;">TEACHER’S ACTIVITIES</th>
            <th style="width: 50%;">STUDENTS’ ACTIVITIES</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>
                - Show 4 sentences on the slide and ask Ss to decide if they are TRUE or FALSE (e.g. finding missing objects after <i>tell</i> or wrong word order).<br>
                - Summarize the lesson: <i>say vs tell</i> and <i>asked if</i>.<br>
                - Homework: Prepare Lesson 10 - Challenge (page 89 - SB).
            </td>
            <td>
                - Read the sentences and call out "True" or "False", correcting the false ones.<br>
                - Write down the homework assignment.
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>

</body>
